MkComponent
  anchor_host_effect_seam
  "The host-effect seam"
  StatusBuilt
  (ConsAnchor anchor_core_ir BaseAnchor)
  (ConsAnchor anchor_spec_index BaseAnchor)
  (ConsSection (MkSection
       anchor_hes_overview
       "The seam"
       InheritSectionStatus
       (ConsBlock (ProseBlock
            (ConsInline (Txt "The")
              (ConsInline (TermDef term_host_effect_seam "host-effect seam")
                (ConsInline (Txt
                     "is the boundary at which the machine offers an effect operation that no source-level handler claims to an ambient host interpreter. It is the earliest bootstrap gate of the native-REPL host handler (ADR-35 D4): a top-level host intercepts a flat effect signature, performs the real operation, and either resumes the delimited continuation with a reply or declines, leaving the machine to take its ordinary step. Its home in the reboot is the host module of crate core-checker.")
                  BaseInline))))
         (ConsBlock (ProseBlock
              (ConsInline (Txt
                   "The seam's whole public vocabulary is three types: a host operation the machine hands out, a")
                (ConsInline (TermDef term_host_reply "host reply")
                  (ConsInline (Txt "the host hands back (resume with a value, or decline), and the")
                    (ConsInline (TermDef term_host_handler "host handler")
                      (ConsInline (Txt
                           "that mediates. The machine offers each host-interceptable perform to a host handler; an unclaimed operation the host also declines blames a no-handler outcome under the machine's ordinary step.")
                        BaseInline))))))
           BaseBlock)))
    (ConsSection (MkSection
         anchor_hes_preserved_boundary
         "The preserved-boundary property"
         InheritSectionStatus
         (ConsBlock (ProseBlock
              (ConsInline (Txt "The seam is a")
                (ConsInlineGlued (TermDef term_preserved_boundary "preserved boundary")
                  (ConsInline (Txt
                       ": it is expressed only over the public value and effect-signature surface and the operation name, never over a machine continuation frame, so it is representation-independent across whichever evaluator realizes it. That representation-independence is what let the seam survive the port from the retired CEK oracle to its successor: the two machines presented the identical seam to the host. The L machine realization (the run-with-host entry of the core-sequent machine) is the durable driver the host runtime binds against; the CEK drivers retired with the CEK at B1 stage F, and the seam outlived them precisely because the boundary never named a machine's internals.")
                    BaseInline))))
           (ConsBlock (ProseBlock
                (ConsInline (Txt
                     "Because the boundary is preserved, the L machine must present the identical seam the earlier machine did: the host observes the same value and effect-signature surface and the same operation names, and is never exposed to the command IL, the two-region store, or any focused-machine state. The seam is therefore the stable contract between the sole driver, the")
                  (ConsInlineGlued (TermRef term_l_machine)
                    (ConsInline (Txt ", and the headless host-effect runtime.")
                      BaseInline))))
             BaseBlock)))
      (ConsSection (MkSection
           anchor_hes_realization
           "Realization and consumers"
           InheritSectionStatus
           (ConsBlock (ProseBlock
                (ConsInline (Txt
                     "The seam types live in core-checker as a distinct boundary concern, beside the newtype vocabulary and the effect-row algebra. The L machine in core-sequent drives them: it offers each unclaimed perform across the structural prefix and resumes or declines. The headless host-effect runtime, crate runtime-host, is the seam's consumer: it binds a host handler over a flat operation signature, performs the syscall-shaped operation, and resumes with the reply. Keeping the seam machine-independent is what lets that runtime bind once and remain correct across machine and arena changes.")
                  BaseInline))
             BaseBlock))
        BaseSection)))
  BaseCiteKey
